1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,170
India.

2
00:00:01,490 --> 00:00:03,870
April sixth, nineteen thirty.

3
00:00:04,190 --> 00:00:07,600
Early morning, on a quiet beach on the west coast.

4
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:14,260
A thin man, sixty years old, wearing a simple white cloth, walks down to the edge of the sea.

5
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:17,180
Thousands of people are watching him.

6
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:20,980
He bends down and picks up a lump of salty mud.

7
00:00:22,004 --> 00:00:27,274
Then he says: with this, I am shaking the foundations of the British Empire.

8
00:00:27,594 --> 00:00:29,404
It sounds impossible.

9
00:00:29,724 --> 00:00:33,754
A handful of salt against the largest empire on Earth.

10
00:00:34,074 --> 00:00:36,444
So how could salt shake an empire?

11
00:00:36,894 --> 00:00:40,694
And how did that walk to the sea end up changing America too?

12
00:00:41,841 --> 00:00:47,181
To find out, we need to understand why picking up salt on a beach was a crime.

13
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:56,290
In nineteen thirty, India was the most valuable part of the British Empire.

14
00:00:56,610 --> 00:01:01,380
More than three hundred million people lived there, and they were ruled from London.

15
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:07,690
Indians had little say in the laws that governed their lives, or in the taxes they paid.

16
00:01:08,709 --> 00:01:12,659
One of those laws was the Salt Act of eighteen eighty-two.

17
00:01:12,979 --> 00:01:16,569
It gave the British government a monopoly on salt.

18
00:01:16,889 --> 00:01:19,919
Indians were not allowed to make it or sell it.

19
00:01:20,239 --> 00:01:26,339
Even on the coast, where salt dried naturally on the rocks, collecting it was a criminal offense.

20
00:01:26,659 --> 00:01:31,239
Everyone had to buy salt from the government, and it came with a tax.

21
00:01:32,258 --> 00:01:40,598
For the poorest families, that tax really hurt, because everyone needs salt, especially in a hot country, working in the fields.

22
00:01:40,918 --> 00:01:45,908
The salt tax made up more than eight percent of the colony's tax income.

23
00:01:46,228 --> 00:01:50,138
And it touched everyone, Hindu and Muslim, rich and poor.

24
00:01:50,458 --> 00:01:56,538
As Gandhi put it, next to air and water, salt is perhaps the greatest necessity of life.

25
00:01:57,556 --> 00:02:03,856
But when Gandhi announced that his great protest would be about salt, many people thought it was a joke.

26
00:02:04,176 --> 00:02:08,356
Even some of his own allies in the Congress party were unsure.

27
00:02:08,676 --> 00:02:12,606
One major newspaper wrote that it was difficult not to laugh.

28
00:02:12,926 --> 00:02:20,996
The Viceroy of India, Lord Irwin, wrote to London that the prospect of a salt campaign did not keep him awake at night.

29
00:02:22,012 --> 00:02:23,652
Gandhi had a plan.

30
00:02:23,972 --> 00:02:35,872
A few weeks earlier, on January twenty-sixth, nineteen thirty, the Indian National Congress had declared that its goal was now complete independence, called Purna Swaraj.

31
00:02:36,192 --> 00:02:40,602
Gandhi was asked to lead the first act of civil disobedience.

32
00:02:40,922 --> 00:02:47,802
He chose salt, because it was something every Indian could understand, and something every Indian could break.

33
00:02:51,619 --> 00:02:55,079
On March second, Gandhi wrote a letter to Lord Irwin.

34
00:02:55,399 --> 00:02:59,749
He listed eleven demands, including abolishing the salt tax.

35
00:03:00,069 --> 00:03:04,359
If they were ignored, he wrote, he would break the salt laws himself.

36
00:03:04,679 --> 00:03:06,459
Irwin did not meet him.

37
00:03:06,779 --> 00:03:12,489
Gandhi said, on bended knees, I asked for bread, and I have received stone instead.

38
00:03:13,507 --> 00:03:17,617
Gandhi did not choose famous politicians to march with him.

39
00:03:17,937 --> 00:03:24,957
He chose seventy-eight men from his own community, the ashram at Sabarmati, trained in strict discipline.

40
00:03:25,277 --> 00:03:29,367
They came from almost every region and religion of India.

41
00:03:29,687 --> 00:03:32,427
And they had one rule above all others.

42
00:03:32,747 --> 00:03:34,027
No violence.

43
00:03:34,347 --> 00:03:36,367
Not even if they were attacked.

44
00:03:40,187 --> 00:03:44,197
On the morning of March twelfth, nineteen thirty, they set out.

45
00:03:44,517 --> 00:03:52,527
Even a newspaper that usually played down Gandhi's crowds reported around a hundred thousand people lining the road.

46
00:03:52,847 --> 00:03:59,767
The goal was the small fishing village of Dandi, on the coast, about two hundred and forty miles away.

47
00:04:00,789 --> 00:04:06,269
The plan was to walk the whole way, about ten miles a day, for twenty-four days.

48
00:04:06,589 --> 00:04:09,859
The route ran south through dozens of villages.

49
00:04:10,179 --> 00:04:14,909
It crossed three great rivers, the Mahi, the Narmada and the Tapi.

50
00:04:15,229 --> 00:04:18,009
At night, the marchers slept in the open.

51
00:04:19,030 --> 00:04:22,940
In every village, crowds came out with drums and cymbals.

52
00:04:23,260 --> 00:04:26,890
Gandhi spoke about the salt tax and about self-rule.

53
00:04:27,210 --> 00:04:32,130
Some village officials resigned from their government jobs on the spot.

54
00:04:32,450 --> 00:04:38,750
New volunteers joined every day, until the column of marchers stretched for about two miles.

55
00:04:39,773 --> 00:04:41,643
And the world was watching.

56
00:04:41,963 --> 00:04:43,963
Gandhi had made sure of that.

57
00:04:44,283 --> 00:04:48,383
Reporters from India, Europe and America followed the march.

58
00:04:48,703 --> 00:04:51,683
Film crews shot newsreels for cinemas.

59
00:04:52,003 --> 00:04:55,753
The New York Times wrote about it almost every day.

60
00:04:56,073 --> 00:05:02,053
A sixty-year-old man walking to the sea had become one of the biggest stories on the planet.

61
00:05:05,871 --> 00:05:09,021
On April fifth, the marchers reached Dandi.

62
00:05:09,341 --> 00:05:12,741
By then, around fifty thousand people had gathered.

63
00:05:13,061 --> 00:05:15,681
The British had not tried to stop them.

64
00:05:16,001 --> 00:05:18,171
Gandhi said he knew why.

65
00:05:18,491 --> 00:05:28,331
The government, he said, was sensitive to world opinion, and the world would not tolerate the repression of a protest that stayed completely peaceful.

66
00:05:29,354 --> 00:05:36,384
The next morning, April sixth, after a prayer, Gandhi walked to the shore and picked up that lump of salty mud.

67
00:05:36,704 --> 00:05:41,204
He boiled it in seawater, making a small amount of illegal salt.

68
00:05:41,524 --> 00:05:43,144
It was a tiny act.

69
00:05:43,464 --> 00:05:47,594
But it was a crime, and he had committed it in front of the whole world.

70
00:05:47,914 --> 00:05:53,194
The poet Sarojini Naidu, marching with him, called out, hail, law breaker.

71
00:05:54,217 --> 00:05:57,387
What happened next surprised even the British.

72
00:05:57,707 --> 00:06:01,247
Millions of Indians began to break the salt law.

73
00:06:01,567 --> 00:06:06,307
People along the coast made salt from seawater and sold it openly.

74
00:06:06,627 --> 00:06:09,507
In the cities, crowds bought illegal salt.

75
00:06:09,827 --> 00:06:15,707
A pinch of salt made by Gandhi himself was auctioned for sixteen hundred rupees.

76
00:06:16,723 --> 00:06:21,783
For the first time, women joined India's freedom struggle in huge numbers.

77
00:06:22,103 --> 00:06:28,833
Gandhi had asked only men to march, but women made salt in their own homes and sold it on the streets.

78
00:06:29,153 --> 00:06:37,153
One activist remembered that even grandmothers carried pots of salt water home, then shouted, we have broken the salt law.

79
00:06:38,175 --> 00:06:39,845
The government struck back.

80
00:06:40,165 --> 00:06:43,525
Over sixty thousand people were sent to prison.

81
00:06:43,845 --> 00:06:45,885
Newspapers were censored.

82
00:06:46,205 --> 00:06:49,295
The Congress party was declared illegal.

83
00:06:49,615 --> 00:06:52,605
But the arrests only made the protest bigger.

84
00:06:53,624 --> 00:06:58,504
Then, just after midnight on May fifth, police came for Gandhi himself.

85
00:06:58,824 --> 00:07:02,354
He was asleep on a cot under the mango trees.

86
00:07:02,674 --> 00:07:06,624
A magistrate arrived with about thirty armed officers.

87
00:07:06,944 --> 00:07:13,944
Gandhi was taken away under an old law from eighteen twenty-seven, and held in prison without a trial.

88
00:07:17,768 --> 00:07:20,628
But Gandhi had already planned his next step.

89
00:07:20,948 --> 00:07:26,488
Not far south of Dandi was a large government salt works at Dharasana.

90
00:07:26,808 --> 00:07:31,818
Volunteers would march to its gates and try to take the salt, peacefully.

91
00:07:32,138 --> 00:07:40,888
The first leaders, a seventy-six-year-old judge named Abbas Tyabji and Gandhi's wife Kasturba, were arrested on the way.

92
00:07:41,208 --> 00:07:44,028
So Sarojini Naidu took over.

93
00:07:45,047 --> 00:07:48,627
Before they set out, she gave the volunteers a warning.

94
00:07:48,947 --> 00:07:52,507
You will be beaten, she said, but you must not resist.

95
00:07:52,827 --> 00:07:55,907
You must not even raise a hand to ward off blows.

96
00:07:56,929 --> 00:08:04,589
On May twenty-first, around two thousand five hundred volunteers walked toward the salt works in silent rows.

97
00:08:04,909 --> 00:08:10,059
Police officers with steel-tipped wooden clubs, called lathis, were waiting.

98
00:08:10,379 --> 00:08:13,229
As each row reached them, the police struck.

99
00:08:13,549 --> 00:08:15,979
The marchers did not fight back.

100
00:08:16,299 --> 00:08:19,489
When one row fell, the next one stepped forward.

101
00:08:20,505 --> 00:08:23,345
An American reporter was standing there.

102
00:08:23,665 --> 00:08:28,395
His name was Webb Miller, and he worked for the United Press news agency.

103
00:08:28,715 --> 00:08:33,895
He wrote that not one of the marchers even raised an arm to fend off the blows.

104
00:08:34,215 --> 00:08:36,615
They went down like ten-pins.

105
00:08:36,935 --> 00:08:40,475
Within minutes, the ground was covered with injured men.

106
00:08:41,498 --> 00:08:47,568
Miller tried to send his story home by telegraph, but British officials in India blocked it.

107
00:08:47,888 --> 00:08:52,658
Only after he threatened to expose the censorship was it allowed through.

108
00:08:52,978 --> 00:08:59,598
His report was printed in about one thousand three hundred and fifty newspapers around the world.

109
00:08:59,918 --> 00:09:05,838
In Washington, a United States senator read it into the official record of the Senate.

110
00:09:09,656 --> 00:09:14,966
At the end of nineteen thirty, Time magazine named Gandhi its Man of the Year.

111
00:09:15,286 --> 00:09:19,986
And Time made a comparison that every American reader understood.

112
00:09:20,306 --> 00:09:26,876
It compared Gandhi's march to the sea to the New Englanders who once defied a British tax on tea.

113
00:09:27,196 --> 00:09:30,616
The Salt March was India's Boston Tea Party.

114
00:09:31,634 --> 00:09:33,654
So did the salt march win?

115
00:09:34,104 --> 00:09:35,454
Not right away.

116
00:09:35,774 --> 00:09:45,604
Early in nineteen thirty-one, Gandhi was released and invited to talk with Lord Irwin, the same Viceroy who once could not be bothered to meet him.

117
00:09:45,924 --> 00:09:49,174
For the first time, the two sat down as equals.

118
00:09:49,494 --> 00:09:54,744
But the British did not give India its freedom, or even abolish the salt tax.

119
00:09:55,760 --> 00:09:58,250
Something deeper had changed, though.

120
00:09:58,570 --> 00:10:02,700
The British Empire ruled India with a few thousand officials.

121
00:10:03,020 --> 00:10:06,680
That only worked as long as Indians went along with it.

122
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:12,030
The salt march showed millions of ordinary people that they could refuse.

123
00:10:12,350 --> 00:10:20,340
As Jawaharlal Nehru later wrote, the real importance of these movements lay in the effect they had on our own people.

124
00:10:20,660 --> 00:10:27,210
Seventeen years later, on August fifteenth, nineteen forty-seven, India became independent.

125
00:10:31,034 --> 00:10:34,074
So, how did a handful of salt shake an empire?

126
00:10:34,524 --> 00:10:35,904
Not with force.

127
00:10:36,224 --> 00:10:43,194
Gandhi chose a law that everyone could break, broke it in front of the whole world, and refused to fight back.

128
00:10:43,514 --> 00:10:46,014
An empire can fight an army.

129
00:10:46,334 --> 00:10:52,044
It is much harder to fight millions of people who simply say no, while the world is watching.

130
00:10:53,062 --> 00:10:55,312
And that brings us to America.

131
00:10:55,632 --> 00:11:02,112
A young pastor in Montgomery, Alabama, named Martin Luther King Junior, read about Gandhi.

132
00:11:02,432 --> 00:11:06,962
King wrote that he was particularly moved by the Salt March to the sea.

133
00:11:07,282 --> 00:11:14,192
During the Montgomery bus boycott, he said that Christ furnished the spirit, while Gandhi furnished the method.

134
00:11:14,512 --> 00:11:23,962
In nineteen fifty-nine, King traveled to India and said, to other countries I may go as a tourist, but to India I come as a pilgrim.

135
00:11:24,982 --> 00:11:36,492
Six years later, in nineteen sixty-five, King and thousands of marchers walked fifty-four miles from Selma to Montgomery, facing police clubs without fighting back.

136
00:11:36,812 --> 00:11:42,682
A march to the sea in India had helped shape a march for the right to vote in America.

137
00:11:43,706 --> 00:11:52,366
Today, the road from Sabarmati to Dandi is a heritage route, and a memorial stands on the beach where Gandhi picked up the salt.

138
00:11:52,686 --> 00:11:55,086
It was only a handful of mud.

139
00:11:55,406 --> 00:12:01,156
But it showed the world that the most powerful thing you can do is sometimes very simple.

140
00:12:01,476 --> 00:12:08,756
If you enjoyed this story, subscribe, and tell us in the comments which moment in history we should tell next.

